---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Breadcrumbs from "../components/Breadcrumbs.astro";
import { site } from "../data/site";
import { getSitePage, REVIEWED_DATE, REVIEWED_ISO } from "../data/sitePages";

const page = getSitePage("/disclaimer/");
---

<BaseLayout
  title={page.title}
  description={page.description}
  alternateMd="/disclaimer/index.md"
  pageType="WebPage"
  dateModified={REVIEWED_ISO}
>
  <Breadcrumbs items={[{ label: "Disclaimer" }]} />
  <section class="tight">
    <div class="wrap narrow prose">
      <h1>Printing information disclaimer</h1>
      <p class="small">Last reviewed: {REVIEWED_DATE}</p>
      <p class="lead" style="font-size:1.05rem;color:var(--ink-2)">
        This site explains how labels and stickers are specified, printed and shipped. It is a buying aid, not a
        substitute for advice from your lawyer, regulatory consultant, customs broker, testing laboratory or market
        authority. The limits below apply to every article, example and calculation on the site.
      </p>

      <h2>1. Information, not professional advice</h2>
      <p>
        Nothing here is legal, regulatory, food-contact, customs, medical or other professional advice. We describe
        common printing questions and the decisions a buyer usually needs to make. We do not tell you whether a
        particular label is lawful in your market, whether a claim about a product is permitted, or whether a
        substance may contact food. Those questions depend on your product, formulation, market, intended use and
        current rules, and they belong with a qualified adviser.
      </p>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th scope="col">What this page does</th><th scope="col">What this page does not do</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Explains printing, material and artwork choices</td><td>Approve a label, product or SKU for a market</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Links or names standards and official sources where relevant</td><td>Interpret a regulation for your product or provide legal advice</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Shows the inputs that affect a quotation and lead time</td><td>Create a binding price or delivery commitment</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Helps you ask better questions of producers and authorities</td><td>Replace confirmation from a regulator, retailer, marketplace or laboratory</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2>2. Artwork and regulatory responsibility sit with the buyer</h2>
      <p>
        The buyer or importer of record is responsible for the content of the artwork: product name, ingredients,
        warnings, net quantity, origin, barcode data, claims, symbols and any language requirements. We print the
        artwork you approve. We can check whether a file is mechanically printable and can flag an obvious missing
        bleed, an unreadable barcode or artwork placed outside a die line. That is a pre-press check, not a legal or
        regulatory review. Approval of a proof confirms that you accept the wording, layout, dimensions and colour
        shown in it.
      </p>

      <h2>3. Regulatory approval is market-specific</h2>
      <p>
        The United States, European Union, United Kingdom, Canada, Australia and every other destination have their
        own rules, and a retailer or marketplace may impose requirements beyond them. A statement that is acceptable
        for one product in one country can be wrong for the same product elsewhere. We do not guarantee that a
        label, package or claim will be accepted by a retailer, marketplace, customs authority or regulator, and
        nothing on this site should be read as that guarantee.
      </p>

      <h2>4. Calculations and examples are illustrative, not a quotation</h2>
      <p>
        Any wrap length, label count, capacity or cost-per-unit figure is an example of the method, not a price for
        your job. A real price depends on final size, shape, material, adhesive, quantity, print method, finishing,
        proofing, packing and freight at the time of quoting. Small changes to a die line, a laminate or a delivery
        postcode can change the result. Only a written quotation issued for your specification is an offer.
      </p>

      <h2>5. Materials, colour and lead times can vary</h2>
      <p>
        Paper, film, adhesive and laminate batches differ. Colour can shift between screen, digital proof, press
        proof and production run, and a screen is never a colour reference. Lead time is counted from approval of
        the final artwork, not from first enquiry, and it can change if material has to be ordered or freight is
        disrupted. The approved proof defines the job; a photograph on a monitor does not. If a particular colour,
        substrate batch or delivery date is essential, ask for it to be agreed in writing and, where appropriate,
        approve a physical sample.
      </p>

      <h2>6. Standards and third-party sources can be revised</h2>
      <p>
        A standard, official guidance page or supplier data sheet may be replaced after we cite it. We link to the
        source we read where a page relies on one, but a citation is not an endorsement of every statement on that
        site and does not freeze the source at the version we used. Before relying on a requirement, open the
        current edition and check its scope. Where a number comes from a supplier data sheet, we identify it as
        supplier data rather than presenting it as our own test.
      </p>

      <h2>7. External links and third-party sites</h2>
      <p>
        Links to registries, standards bodies, carriers, authorities and other third parties are provided for
        verification and context. We do not control those sites, their availability, their security or their
        accuracy, and a link does not create a partnership or approval. If an external page conflicts with this
        site, the official source and your written contract should take priority.
      </p>

      <h2>8. No guarantee of commercial outcome</h2>
      <p>
        We do not promise sales, search rankings, marketplace approval, retailer acceptance, customer conversion or
        any other commercial result. A well-printed label cannot make a product compliant or successful, and a
        technically correct proof cannot guarantee that a third party will interpret it as you intend. Those
        outcomes depend on factors outside the print job.
      </p>

      <h2>9. Limitation of liability</h2>
      <p>
        To the extent permitted by law, we are not liable for decisions made solely on the basis of general
        information on this website. Liability for an order is governed by our <a href="/terms/">terms of sale</a>,
        which limit it to the value of the affected order and exclude indirect or consequential loss. Nothing in
        those terms or this page excludes a right or liability that cannot lawfully be excluded, including
        mandatory consumer rights where they apply.
      </p>

      <h2>10. Five checks before you rely on a page</h2>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Check the date.</strong> Read the “Last reviewed” line and ask whether rules or materials may have changed since then.</li>
        <li><strong>Open the cited source.</strong> Read the current version, not only the sentence that quotes it.</li>
        <li><strong>Confirm with the authority or marketplace.</strong> Check the requirement with the body that will enforce it, in the market where your product will be sold.</li>
        <li><strong>Get written confirmation for your SKU and market.</strong> A generic answer is not approval of your product, artwork or container.</li>
        <li><strong>Tell us if something is wrong.</strong> Send the page, the sentence and your evidence so we can review it.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2>11. How to report an error</h2>
      <p>
        Email <a href={`mailto:${site.email}`}>{site.email}</a> with the subject “Correction” and include the page
        URL, the passage, what you believe is wrong and a source we can check. Our <a href="/corrections/">corrections
        policy</a> explains the response times and how a change is recorded. A disagreement with a recommendation is
        also useful: it tells us where a page needs to be clearer.
      </p>
      <p class="small">
        Related policies: <a href="/privacy/">Privacy</a> · <a href="/cookies/">Cookies</a> ·
        <a href="/terms/">Terms</a> · <a href="/refund-policy/">Returns</a> ·
        <a href="/shipping-delivery/">Shipping</a> · <a href="/declarations/">Declarations</a> ·
        <a href="/editorial-standards/">Editorial standards</a> · <a href="/methodology/">Methodology</a> ·
        <a href="/corrections/">Corrections</a> · <a href="/accessibility/">Accessibility</a> ·
        <a href="/contact/">Contact</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
